finding: Staged discontinuities survive an intervening fixed tick after an abort and wrongly snap the newer tick's placement. GlobalTransformHistoryStartSystem clears TransformRuntime.discontinuities at tick start but does not clear historyDiscontinuitiesOnSubmit. stageHistoryDiscontinuities then ORs that old staged bit into the next preparation, and copyPhase copies the new current row over previous because of a discontinuity that belonged to the earlier tick. Independent reproduction on HEAD in an isolated copy using placementHistoryApp: submit x=10; in the next fixed writer set x=20 and teleport(world,eid), then abort that draw; in the following fixed tick set x=30 without teleport and submit. GPU readback of previous is 30, expected 20. The uninterrupted control with exactly those two fixed writes and the teleport returns previous=20. Thus the extra abort makes the later ordinary movement snap instead of interpolate. The retained tests cover abort followed by step(0), not a new fixed tick after an aborted discontinuity. 46 narrow HEAD tests pass, including the late-mesh registration regression, GlobalTransform owner tests, engine callback/disposal tests, cluster/reload, point-atlas, and tick/table-upload allocation files. Source review finds restoration clears staged marks and array growth preserves old staged words with allocation only when the live mark array outgrows capacity; those do not repair the tick-boundary failure. No worktree source edits or commits were made.
